Member-only story

Manual subscriptions often create duplicate state, extra cleanup, and synchronization problems. Modern Angular gives you cleaner ways to connect streams to the UI.

4 min read
Just now

--

Press enter or click to view image in full size

The Pattern Looks Harmless
A component needs customer data from an API.
The service returns an Observable:
readonly customers$ = this.customerApi.getCustomers();The component subscribes and copies the result into a Signal:
export class CustomerPageComponent {
private readonly customerService =
inject(CustomerService);readonly customers = signal<Customer[]>([]);
ngOnInit(): void {
this.customerService.customers$
.subscribe(customers => {
this.customers.set(customers);
});
}
}
This works.
But the component has now created another state container for data that already exists in the Observable.
The Hidden Cost
The component now owns:

• The subscription

• The copied Signal

• The synchronization between both values
